00:28:15.159 --> 00:28:18.240
[music]

00:28:16.399 --> 00:28:20.799
Researching a stock is what separates

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:22.720
the investors from the gamblers. And the

00:28:20.799 --> 00:28:25.279
people who lose the most usually skip

00:28:22.720 --> 00:28:27.600
this stage and buy on a gut feeling or

00:28:25.279 --> 00:28:29.600
because the stock is already flying. So

00:28:27.600 --> 00:28:31.200
let me show you the two main ways that

00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:33.679
you can research a stock before

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:36.399
investing. The first is something called

00:28:33.679 --> 00:28:38.640
technical analysis, which is essentially

00:28:36.399 --> 00:28:41.039
reading the patterns in the charts. The

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:42.880
basic idea is actually pretty simple.

00:28:41.039 --> 00:28:44.799
Technical analysts believe that the

00:28:42.880 --> 00:28:47.120
information people use to buy and sell a

00:28:44.799 --> 00:28:49.039
stock eventually shows up in its price

00:28:47.120 --> 00:28:52.080
because markets are driven by human

00:28:49.039 --> 00:28:54.080
behavior. Things like fear, greed,

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:56.240
optimism, and panic, which create

00:28:54.080 --> 00:28:57.600
patterns that repeat over time. So

00:28:56.240 --> 00:28:59.679
rather than trying to work out

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:02.240
everything happening inside the economy,

00:28:59.679 --> 00:29:04.399
a technical trader is mainly asking one

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:06.720
question. What is the current price

00:29:04.399 --> 00:29:08.880
telling me? There are four things they

00:29:06.720 --> 00:29:11.039
pay particular close attention to. The

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:13.120
first is the trend which is simply the

00:29:11.039 --> 00:29:15.520
overall direction the price is moving.

00:29:13.120 --> 00:29:17.919
In an uptrend, the price generally keeps

00:29:15.520 --> 00:29:20.399
reaching new highs while its pullbacks

00:29:17.919 --> 00:29:22.720
also stay higher than before. In a

00:29:20.399 --> 00:29:24.559
downtrend, the opposite happens. The

00:29:22.720 --> 00:29:26.480
highs and the pullbacks keep moving

00:29:24.559 --> 00:29:28.559
lower. One of the basic principles of

00:29:26.480 --> 00:29:31.039
technical analysis is that you generally

00:29:28.559 --> 00:29:32.559
don't want to fight a strong trend. If a

00:29:31.039 --> 00:29:34.799
stock has been moving upwards for

00:29:32.559 --> 00:29:36.799
months, for example, a technical trader

00:29:34.799 --> 00:29:38.720
may look for an opportunity to join that

00:29:36.799 --> 00:29:40.399
trend rather than immediately betting

00:29:38.720 --> 00:29:42.640
that it's going to reverse. The second

00:29:40.399 --> 00:29:44.480
is support and resistance. These are

00:29:42.640 --> 00:29:46.559
basically price levels where buyers and

00:29:44.480 --> 00:29:49.039
sellers have repeatedly stepped in.

00:29:46.559 --> 00:29:50.880
Support is like the floor. It's an area

00:29:49.039 --> 00:29:52.720
where a falling stock has previously

00:29:50.880 --> 00:29:54.880
found enough buyers to stop the price

00:29:52.720 --> 00:29:57.440
falling further and push it back up.

00:29:54.880 --> 00:29:59.600
Resistance is like a ceiling. It's where

00:29:57.440 --> 00:30:01.360
a rising stock has previously found

00:29:59.600 --> 00:30:03.440
enough sellers to stop it going higher

00:30:01.360 --> 00:30:06.000
and push it back down. If a stock keeps

00:30:03.440 --> 00:30:09.360
falling towards $80 and then bouncing

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:12.240
back up, traders might identify $80 as

00:30:09.360 --> 00:30:15.679
an area of support. And if it repeatedly

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:18.399
rises towards $100 before falling back,

00:30:15.679 --> 00:30:20.080
$100 could be an area of resistance. If

00:30:18.399 --> 00:30:22.399
the price eventually breaks through one

00:30:20.080 --> 00:30:24.320
of these two levels, traders pay close

00:30:22.399 --> 00:30:26.159
attention because it can signal that the

00:30:24.320 --> 00:30:28.559
balance between buyers and sellers has

00:30:26.159 --> 00:30:30.640
changed. The third is volume, which is

00:30:28.559 --> 00:30:32.559
the number of shares being traded. And

00:30:30.640 --> 00:30:34.000
it helps traders understand how many

00:30:32.559 --> 00:30:36.480
people are involved in the price

00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:38.799
movement. For example, if a stock jumps

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:41.760
10% while very few shares are being

00:30:38.799 --> 00:30:44.159
traded, that's very different from a 10%

00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:46.399
jump accomplished by a huge increase in

00:30:44.159 --> 00:30:48.320
trading volume. Technical traders look

00:30:46.399 --> 00:30:50.559
at volume alongside the price to help

00:30:48.320 --> 00:30:53.520
judge how significant a move might be.

00:30:50.559 --> 00:30:55.520
And fourth is moving averages. A moving

00:30:53.520 --> 00:30:57.279
average is basically a way of smoothing

00:30:55.520 --> 00:31:00.000
out the price so you can see the

00:30:57.279 --> 00:31:02.799
underlying trend more clearly. Take a

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:04.960
50-day moving average for example. It

00:31:02.799 --> 00:31:07.520
calculates the average price over the

00:31:04.960 --> 00:31:10.000
most recent 50 trading days and then

00:31:07.520 --> 00:31:11.919
keeps updating as each new trading day

00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:14.320
comes along. So that's technical

00:31:11.919 --> 00:31:16.320
analysis in a nutshell. But if I'm being

00:31:14.320 --> 00:31:18.799
honest with you, this strategy is more

00:31:16.320 --> 00:31:20.880
for active traders and people almost

00:31:18.799 --> 00:31:22.799
investing as some kind of side hustle or

00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:25.279
full-time job. If you're a long-term

00:31:22.799 --> 00:31:27.679
investor like me, day-to-day price

00:31:25.279 --> 00:31:29.679
movements of a stock or ETF shouldn't

00:31:27.679 --> 00:31:32.080
make a huge difference to you. What does

00:31:29.679 --> 00:31:35.120
matter is the second way to research a

00:31:32.080 --> 00:31:37.120
stock. Fundamental analysis. This is

00:31:35.120 --> 00:31:38.880
where you stop looking primarily at the

00:31:37.120 --> 00:31:41.120
chart and start looking at the actual

00:31:38.880 --> 00:31:43.360
business behind the stock. Because when

00:31:41.120 --> 00:31:45.840
you buy a share, you're buying a small

00:31:43.360 --> 00:31:48.720
piece of a real company. A fundamental

00:31:45.840 --> 00:31:51.600
investor is really trying to answer two

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:54.080
questions. Is this a good business? And

00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:56.159
am I paying a sensible price for it? And

00:31:54.080 --> 00:31:58.240
to answer those questions, you need to

00:31:56.159 --> 00:32:00.480
understand both the business itself, and

00:31:58.240 --> 00:32:01.919
the numbers behind it. First, you need

00:32:00.480 --> 00:32:04.159
to understand what you're actually

00:32:01.919 --> 00:32:06.720
buying by considering what the company

00:32:04.159 --> 00:32:08.799
sells, who its customers are, how they

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:10.559
make money, and why customers choose

00:32:08.799 --> 00:32:12.799
this particular business over its

00:32:10.559 --> 00:32:14.799
competition. If you can't explain how a

00:32:12.799 --> 00:32:17.039
company makes money in simple terms,

00:32:14.799 --> 00:32:20.559
then you probably shouldn't be investing

00:32:17.039 --> 00:32:22.799
in it. Second, dive into the financials.

00:32:20.559 --> 00:32:24.240
And this is where fundamental analysis

00:32:22.799 --> 00:32:25.919
actually starts to get really

00:32:24.240 --> 00:32:27.440
interesting because you're trying to

00:32:25.919 --> 00:32:29.120
work out whether the business is

00:32:27.440 --> 00:32:30.960
actually performing well or not.

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:33.360
Essentially, you're looking at things

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:35.840
like is the revenue growing? Are the

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:38.000
profits growing? Are the profit margins

00:32:35.840 --> 00:32:40.000
increasing? And how much debt does it

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:41.600
have? You don't need to understand every

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:43.519
number in the company's financial

00:32:41.600 --> 00:32:45.200
statement, but there are a handful of

00:32:43.519 --> 00:32:47.200
key metrics that can tell you a lot

00:32:45.200 --> 00:32:48.960
about the health and performance of a

00:32:47.200 --> 00:32:51.360
business. I'll break them down for you

00:32:48.960 --> 00:32:53.200
in just a second. Third, work out

00:32:51.360 --> 00:32:55.519
whether the business has a competitive

00:32:53.200 --> 00:32:58.240
advantage. This is what investors often

00:32:55.519 --> 00:33:00.320
call a moat. Imagine you found a company

00:32:58.240 --> 00:33:03.200
that's growing quickly and making huge

00:33:00.320 --> 00:33:05.840
profits. What's stopping another company

00:33:03.200 --> 00:33:08.240
from copying it? If the answer is not

00:33:05.840 --> 00:33:10.159
much, those profits could disappear a

00:33:08.240 --> 00:33:12.559
lot quicker than you might think. The Mo

00:33:10.159 --> 00:33:14.960
could be a powerful brand, patents, a

00:33:12.559 --> 00:33:16.880
huge cost advantage, or customers who

00:33:14.960 --> 00:33:20.399
find it difficult or expensive to

00:33:16.880 --> 00:33:22.480
switch. But if I'm being honest, I'd put

00:33:20.399 --> 00:33:24.880
the most emphasis on things like patents

00:33:22.480 --> 00:33:26.480
that are almost impossible to copy

00:33:24.880 --> 00:33:28.399
because something like a huge cost

00:33:26.480 --> 00:33:30.399
advantage can eventually be beaten by

00:33:28.399 --> 00:33:32.320
the competition. Fourth, look at the

00:33:30.399 --> 00:33:34.399
management. A business can have great

00:33:32.320 --> 00:33:36.240
products and loads of profit, but you

00:33:34.399 --> 00:33:38.559
still need people who can allocate that

00:33:36.240 --> 00:33:40.480
profit correctly, otherwise they might

00:33:38.559 --> 00:33:42.640
waste it all. Look at the people running

00:33:40.480 --> 00:33:44.960
it and think, do they make sensible

00:33:42.640 --> 00:33:47.039
investments? Do they use their profits

00:33:44.960 --> 00:33:49.919
effectively? Do they communicate

00:33:47.039 --> 00:33:52.080
honestly with shareholders? And do they

00:33:49.919 --> 00:33:54.080
actually create value for the owners of

00:33:52.080 --> 00:33:56.399
the business? And fifth, look at the

00:33:54.080 --> 00:33:58.399
company's valuation. This is actually

00:33:56.399 --> 00:34:00.159
really important because you can find an

00:33:58.399 --> 00:34:02.399
absolutely brilliant company doing

00:34:00.159 --> 00:34:04.799
amazing things and it still makes a

00:34:02.399 --> 00:34:08.480
terrible investment. Imagine I offer you

00:34:04.799 --> 00:34:10.879
a $100 bill and you pay me $500 for it.

00:34:08.480 --> 00:34:13.440
The $100 bill hasn't suddenly become a

00:34:10.879 --> 00:34:15.359
bad asset. You've just paid five times

00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:17.280
what it's worth. The same thing can

00:34:15.359 --> 00:34:19.599
happen in the stock market. A company

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:22.240
can be growing very quickly, have great

00:34:19.599 --> 00:34:24.240
profit margins and almost no debt. But

00:34:22.240 --> 00:34:26.159
if everybody already knows that and

00:34:24.240 --> 00:34:28.320
investors have pushed the share price to

00:34:26.159 --> 00:34:30.079
an inflated level, the stock might be

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:32.320
overpriced. This is where you'll hear

00:34:30.079 --> 00:34:35.760
investors talk about things like the PE

00:34:32.320 --> 00:34:38.240
ratio. PE stands for price to earnings,

00:34:35.760 --> 00:34:40.320
and it sounds complicated, but in simple

00:34:38.240 --> 00:34:41.919
terms, it tells you how much investors

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:44.159
are paying for each dollar of the

00:34:41.919 --> 00:34:47.440
company's earnings. So, if a company has

00:34:44.159 --> 00:34:50.639
a PE of 20, investors are paying roughly

00:34:47.440 --> 00:34:52.960
$20 for every $1 of annual earnings. But

00:34:50.639 --> 00:34:54.960
a PE ratio on its own doesn't really

00:34:52.960 --> 00:34:57.200
tell you whether a stock is cheap or

00:34:54.960 --> 00:35:00.000
expensive. Because a company growing its

00:34:57.200 --> 00:35:02.720
earnings by 30% a year might reasonably

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:04.880
trade at a much higher PE than a company

00:35:02.720 --> 00:35:07.200
whose earnings aren't growing at all.

00:35:04.880 --> 00:35:09.280
So, you need context. This is exactly

00:35:07.200 --> 00:35:12.079
why there are other valuation measures

00:35:09.280 --> 00:35:14.400
investors use such as price to sales,

00:35:12.079 --> 00:35:17.280
price to free cash flow, and enterprise

00:35:14.400 --> 00:35:19.200
value to EBIT DAR. You don't need to

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:20.960
memorize all of them right now. The

00:35:19.200 --> 00:35:22.880
important thing is just understanding

00:35:20.960 --> 00:35:25.359
the principle. That's what fundamental

00:35:22.880 --> 00:35:27.200
analysis really is. You're combining the

00:35:25.359 --> 00:35:29.280
story of the business with the numbers

00:35:27.200 --> 00:35:31.359
behind it, which makes a lot more sense

00:35:29.280 --> 00:35:32.720
if you're a long-term investor like me.

00:35:31.359 --> 00:35:34.320
But look, you don't need to get

00:35:32.720 --> 00:35:36.079
overwhelmed by all of this. And of

00:35:34.320 --> 00:35:38.640
course, you can come back to this video

