00:43:17.440 --> 00:43:21.680
I mentioned bonds when explaining the

00:43:19.440 --> 00:43:23.920
three fund portfolio, but let me finally

00:43:21.680 --> 00:43:25.599
give them their moment because they play

00:43:23.920 --> 00:43:27.280
quite an important role in keeping you

00:43:25.599 --> 00:43:29.440
calm when everything else in your

00:43:27.280 --> 00:43:31.520
portfolio is on fire. A bond is

00:43:29.440 --> 00:43:33.839
basically just a loan issued by either a

00:43:31.520 --> 00:43:35.359
company or more commonly a government.

00:43:33.839 --> 00:43:37.280
When a government wants to spend more

00:43:35.359 --> 00:43:39.280
than it collects in taxes, it doesn't

00:43:37.280 --> 00:43:41.920
ring a bank and instead just writes an

00:43:39.280 --> 00:43:44.400
IOU and sells it to investors all over

00:43:41.920 --> 00:43:46.079
the world. Essentially, you hand the

00:43:44.400 --> 00:43:48.319
government your money now and they

00:43:46.079 --> 00:43:50.960
promise to pay you back later with a bit

00:43:48.319 --> 00:43:52.640
of interest on top. And that interest is

00:43:50.960 --> 00:43:54.160
known as the yield. There are a few

00:43:52.640 --> 00:43:55.920
different types of bonds depending on

00:43:54.160 --> 00:43:58.560
how long you're lending the money for.

00:43:55.920 --> 00:44:01.040
In the US, for example, treasury bills

00:43:58.560 --> 00:44:03.680
are shortterm, generally lasting a year

00:44:01.040 --> 00:44:06.240
or less. Treasury notes typically last

00:44:03.680 --> 00:44:09.440
between 2 and 10 years, while treasury

00:44:06.240 --> 00:44:11.440
bonds can run for 20 or 30 years. Now,

00:44:09.440 --> 00:44:12.960
you might be wondering, does buying a

00:44:11.440 --> 00:44:15.680
bond mean your money is completely

00:44:12.960 --> 00:44:17.680
locked away until that date? Well, not

00:44:15.680 --> 00:44:19.680
necessarily. If you hold a treasury

00:44:17.680 --> 00:44:21.440
until it matures, the government pays

00:44:19.680 --> 00:44:24.000
you back your original investment,

00:44:21.440 --> 00:44:26.640
assuming they don't default. But you can

00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:28.480
also sell most bonds before they mature.

00:44:26.640 --> 00:44:30.000
But their market price can move up and

00:44:28.480 --> 00:44:31.920
down while you own them. This is where

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:33.920
the yield really comes in because the

00:44:31.920 --> 00:44:35.760
yield is essentially the return you're

00:44:33.920 --> 00:44:38.560
getting from the bond at its current

00:44:35.760 --> 00:44:41.119
market price and bond prices and yields

00:44:38.560 --> 00:44:43.200
generally move in opposite directions.

00:44:41.119 --> 00:44:45.680
So if new bonds start paying higher

00:44:43.200 --> 00:44:47.839
rates, your older lower paying bonds

00:44:45.680 --> 00:44:51.319
become less attractive and their price

00:44:47.839 --> 00:44:51.319
usually falls.

