00:49:37.200 --> 00:49:40.400
of this next style. Now, I know I

00:49:39.040 --> 00:49:42.480
touched a little bit on dividends

00:49:40.400 --> 00:49:44.480
earlier, but let's dive a bit deeper.

00:49:42.480 --> 00:49:46.160
Dividend investing is a very popular

00:49:44.480 --> 00:49:48.000
strategy, and some investors

00:49:46.160 --> 00:49:50.079
intentionally build portfolios around

00:49:48.000 --> 00:49:52.160
companies with a long history of paying

00:49:50.079 --> 00:49:53.680
and increasing those dividends. You'd

00:49:52.160 --> 00:49:55.680
often hear of these companies being

00:49:53.680 --> 00:49:58.240
described as dividend aristocrats or

00:49:55.680 --> 00:49:59.520
dividend kings. Dividend aristocrats are

00:49:58.240 --> 00:50:02.079
companies that have increased their

00:49:59.520 --> 00:50:04.640
dividends every year for at least 25

00:50:02.079 --> 00:50:07.280
consecutive years. And dividend kings

00:50:04.640 --> 00:50:09.839
take that even further with at least 50

00:50:07.280 --> 00:50:12.319
consecutive years of dividend increases.

00:50:09.839 --> 00:50:14.000
Think about what that actually means. A

00:50:12.319 --> 00:50:15.920
company has managed to keep increasing

00:50:14.000 --> 00:50:18.640
the amount of money it pay shareholders

00:50:15.920 --> 00:50:21.280
through recessions, inflation, market

00:50:18.640 --> 00:50:23.520
crashes, and all sorts of chaos, which

00:50:21.280 --> 00:50:25.200
definitely signals a strong company. Of

00:50:23.520 --> 00:50:27.280
course, that doesn't automatically make

00:50:25.200 --> 00:50:28.720
it a great investment, but it does tell

00:50:27.280 --> 00:50:31.119
you something about the company's

00:50:28.720 --> 00:50:33.280
history of generating cash and returning

00:50:31.119 --> 00:50:35.200
it to its shareholders. Now, if you like

00:50:33.280 --> 00:50:37.200
the sound of dividend investing, there's

00:50:35.200 --> 00:50:39.760
an important number you really need to

00:50:37.200 --> 00:50:41.520
pay attention to. The dividend yield.

00:50:39.760 --> 00:50:43.599
It's the amount of money you'll be paid

00:50:41.520 --> 00:50:46.319
annually displayed as a percentage of

00:50:43.599 --> 00:50:48.960
the share price. For example, if we look

00:50:46.319 --> 00:50:51.680
at CocaCola, because they've increased

00:50:48.960 --> 00:50:55.920
dividends for over 60 years. And if we

00:50:51.680 --> 00:51:00.000
head over to the financials tab, we can

00:50:55.920 --> 00:51:02.240
see that they're paying a 2.36%

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:04.720
dividend yield, which means if you own

00:51:02.240 --> 00:51:07.359
10 shares of Coca-Cola, you'd receive

00:51:04.720 --> 00:51:09.359
roughly $21 a year in dividends without

00:51:07.359 --> 00:51:11.040
ever having to sell the stock. But it's

00:51:09.359 --> 00:51:13.040
important to remember that a higher

00:51:11.040 --> 00:51:15.359
yield doesn't automatically make it a

00:51:13.040 --> 00:51:17.040
better investment. A 3% yield from a

00:51:15.359 --> 00:51:19.359
company that's steadily increasing its

00:51:17.040 --> 00:51:21.760
dividend and turning a profit can be a

00:51:19.359 --> 00:51:23.599
much better investment than a 10% yield

00:51:21.760 --> 00:51:25.200
from a struggling company whose share

00:51:23.599 --> 00:51:26.960
price is collapsing. That's why

00:51:25.200 --> 00:51:28.880
experienced dividend investors look

00:51:26.960 --> 00:51:31.200
beyond a yield and look at a company's

00:51:28.880 --> 00:51:33.040
payout ratio, which tells you roughly

00:51:31.200 --> 00:51:35.680
how much of its profits are being paid

00:51:33.040 --> 00:51:38.480
out as dividends. If a company earns $10

00:51:35.680 --> 00:51:41.839
per share and pays 4% in dividends, its

00:51:38.480 --> 00:51:44.160
payout ratio is 40%. That leaves the

00:51:41.839 --> 00:51:46.640
company with the other 60% to reinvest

00:51:44.160 --> 00:51:48.800
in the business, pay down debt, buy

00:51:46.640 --> 00:51:50.800
other companies, or buy back their own

00:51:48.800 --> 00:51:53.040
shares. The exact number that's healthy

00:51:50.800 --> 00:51:55.040
depends heavily on the industry. But the

00:51:53.040 --> 00:51:56.800
basic idea is that you want to know

00:51:55.040 --> 00:51:59.280
whether the dividend is actually

00:51:56.800 --> 00:52:01.680
sustainable. Because let's be real, a

00:51:59.280 --> 00:52:03.599
dividend isn't just free money. And when

00:52:01.680 --> 00:52:05.040
a company pays one, that money is

00:52:03.599 --> 00:52:06.960
leaving the company and going to

00:52:05.040 --> 00:52:09.839
shareholders. So, you shouldn't look at

00:52:06.960 --> 00:52:11.839
it as some magical extra return on top

00:52:09.839 --> 00:52:14.319
of the company's profit. This is exactly

00:52:11.839 --> 00:52:16.559
why I pay close attention to the total

00:52:14.319 --> 00:52:18.559
return, which is a combination of the

00:52:16.559 --> 00:52:20.640
change in its share price and the

00:52:18.559 --> 00:52:23.359
dividends you receive. A company paying

00:52:20.640 --> 00:52:26.000
a 5% dividend isn't necessarily better

00:52:23.359 --> 00:52:27.599
than a company only paying 1% if the

00:52:26.000 --> 00:52:29.920
first company's share price keeps

00:52:27.599 --> 00:52:31.839
falling while the second one is growing.

00:52:29.920 --> 00:52:33.520
So, if you're interested in dividend

00:52:31.839 --> 00:52:35.839
investing, don't just hunt for the

00:52:33.520 --> 00:52:38.720
biggest yields you can find. Look for a

00:52:35.839 --> 00:52:41.200
healthy business, sustainable cash flow,

00:52:38.720 --> 00:52:43.760
manageable debt, a sensible payout

00:52:41.200 --> 00:52:46.480
ratio, and a history of returning money

00:52:43.760 --> 00:52:49.581
to shareholders. Okay, let's move on to

00:52:46.480 --> 00:52:51.601
the wild west of the investing world.

00:52:49.581 --> 00:52:51.601
[music]

00:52:52.480 --> 00:52:57.200
Right, let me give you my honest take on

00:52:55.200 --> 00:52:59.839
crypto because it's probably the most

00:52:57.200 --> 00:53:02.079
misunderstood but also risky asset we've

00:52:59.839 --> 00:53:04.400
talked about so far. To put it simply,

00:53:02.079 --> 00:53:06.640
crypto is a digital asset that uses a

00:53:04.400 --> 00:53:08.960
technology called blockchain to record

00:53:06.640 --> 00:53:10.960
who owns what and who's sending what to

00:53:08.960 --> 00:53:12.880
who. The big difference between crypto

00:53:10.960 --> 00:53:14.880
and money sitting in your bank account

00:53:12.880 --> 00:53:17.200
is that crypto can operate without a

00:53:14.880 --> 00:53:18.880
central bank or traditional financial

00:53:17.200 --> 00:53:21.119
institution sitting in the middle of

00:53:18.880 --> 00:53:23.599
every transaction. Let's take Bitcoin

00:53:21.119 --> 00:53:26.079
for example. Normally, if I send you

00:53:23.599 --> 00:53:29.760
$100, a bank keeps a ledger that says,

00:53:26.079 --> 00:53:32.480
"I had $100, you received $100, and now

00:53:29.760 --> 00:53:34.480
I have $100 less." Bitcoin essentially

00:53:32.480 --> 00:53:36.880
replaces that central ledger with a

00:53:34.480 --> 00:53:38.960
decentralized network of computers.

00:53:36.880 --> 00:53:40.960
Transactions are recorded on a public

00:53:38.960 --> 00:53:43.359
blockchain, and the network uses

00:53:40.960 --> 00:53:45.200
cryptography and a consensus system to

00:53:43.359 --> 00:53:47.520
agree on what's legit. That's actually

00:53:45.200 --> 00:53:49.760
where the word crypto comes from. This

00:53:47.520 --> 00:53:51.839
is important because Bitcoin isn't just

00:53:49.760 --> 00:53:54.400
a digital version of a dollar sitting in

00:53:51.839 --> 00:53:56.480
your bank account. Its entire system was

00:53:54.400 --> 00:53:59.599
designed around having a fixed maximum

00:53:56.480 --> 00:54:01.599
supply of 21 million coins. New Bitcoin

00:53:59.599 --> 00:54:03.599
is released into circulation from a

00:54:01.599 --> 00:54:05.920
process called mining. And the amount

00:54:03.599 --> 00:54:07.599
being mined gradually falls over time.

00:54:05.920 --> 00:54:10.160
That's one of the main reasons people

00:54:07.599 --> 00:54:12.319
compare Bitcoin to digital gold because

00:54:10.160 --> 00:54:14.480
it's an asset that isn't controlled by a

00:54:12.319 --> 00:54:16.160
single government and its supply isn't

00:54:14.480 --> 00:54:18.800
something a central bank can increase

00:54:16.160 --> 00:54:21.040
whenever it wants. But Bitcoin isn't the

00:54:18.800 --> 00:54:23.119
only type of crypto. You've also got

00:54:21.040 --> 00:54:25.040
networks like Ethereum which are trying

00:54:23.119 --> 00:54:27.280
to do something a little bit different.

00:54:25.040 --> 00:54:29.280
Ethereum isn't just designed to be money

00:54:27.280 --> 00:54:31.040
because its blockchain can run programs

00:54:29.280 --> 00:54:32.480
called smart contracts, which are

00:54:31.040 --> 00:54:34.800
essentially pieces of code that

00:54:32.480 --> 00:54:36.720
automatically execute when certain

00:54:34.800 --> 00:54:39.119
conditions are met. Look, there are

00:54:36.720 --> 00:54:40.720
thousands of other cryptocurrencies from

00:54:39.119 --> 00:54:43.280
legitimate projects with different

00:54:40.720 --> 00:54:46.000
purposes to coins that are basically

00:54:43.280 --> 00:54:48.079
speculation fueled by nothing more than

00:54:46.000 --> 00:54:49.599
hype. So, when you're looking at crypto,

00:54:48.079 --> 00:54:51.920
you need to understand what you're

00:54:49.599 --> 00:54:54.160
actually buying. Because unlike a normal

00:54:51.920 --> 00:54:56.559
company, you can't look at a crypto

00:54:54.160 --> 00:54:58.880
token and see revenue, profit, cash

00:54:56.559 --> 00:55:00.880
flow, and dividends in the same way,

00:54:58.880 --> 00:55:03.040
which is one of the main reasons valuing

00:55:00.880 --> 00:55:05.520
crypto is so difficult. Which brings me

00:55:03.040 --> 00:55:08.240
on to the risk. Now, I personally do

00:55:05.520 --> 00:55:10.240
invest in crypto, but it's a very small

00:55:08.240 --> 00:55:13.520
percentage of my portfolio because

00:55:10.240 --> 00:55:16.079
crypto can move 20% or 30% in a single

00:55:13.520 --> 00:55:18.000
day, and some smaller coins move even

00:55:16.079 --> 00:55:20.400
more than that. Prices are heavily

00:55:18.000 --> 00:55:22.319
influenced by sentiment, speculation,

00:55:20.400 --> 00:55:24.160
interest rates, and how much money is

00:55:22.319 --> 00:55:25.760
flowing into the market. And this is

00:55:24.160 --> 00:55:27.920
where I think people get crypto

00:55:25.760 --> 00:55:30.400
completely wrong. They see something go

00:55:27.920 --> 00:55:32.400
up 500% and think the lesson is that

00:55:30.400 --> 00:55:34.480
they should have bought more. But the

00:55:32.400 --> 00:55:37.599
actual lesson is that an asset capable

00:55:34.480 --> 00:55:39.440
of going up 500% can also be capable of

00:55:37.599 --> 00:55:41.359
wiping you out completely. In my

00:55:39.440 --> 00:55:44.160
opinion, as someone who's been investing

00:55:41.359 --> 00:55:46.880
successfully for over 40 years, crypto

00:55:44.160 --> 00:55:49.280
is a speculative part of any portfolio

00:55:46.880 --> 00:55:50.880
and definitely not a foundation. But if

00:55:49.280 --> 00:55:53.119
you understand what you're buying,

00:55:50.880 --> 00:55:55.040
accept the possibility of huge losses

00:55:53.119 --> 00:55:57.280
and want some exposure to the potential

00:55:55.040 --> 00:55:58.960
upside, a small allocation can

00:55:57.280 --> 00:56:00.559
definitely make sense. But the goal

00:55:58.960 --> 00:56:02.880
shouldn't be to find the next coin

00:56:00.559 --> 00:56:05.040
that's going to go up 100x. Because

00:56:02.880 --> 00:56:07.119
investing isn't about finding the asset

00:56:05.040 --> 00:56:09.040
that can make you the most money. It's

00:56:07.119 --> 00:56:10.960
about building a portfolio that can

00:56:09.040 --> 00:56:13.280
survive the things you don't see coming

00:56:10.960 --> 00:56:15.280
and build you wealth over time. Okay.

00:56:13.280 --> 00:56:17.599
So, now you understand all the different

00:56:15.280 --> 00:56:19.599
assets available to invest in. The next

00:56:17.599 --> 00:56:22.000
step is to build a portfolio that works

00:56:19.599 --> 00:56:24.423
for you, your age, and your risk

00:56:22.000 --> 00:56:26.319
tolerance.

00:56:24.423 --> 00:56:28.160
[music]

00:56:26.319 --> 00:56:30.319
Investing definitely isn't the same for

00:56:28.160 --> 00:56:32.160
everyone. And if you're in your 20ies,

00:56:30.319 --> 00:56:34.160
your strategy probably won't look the

00:56:32.160 --> 00:56:35.839
same as [music] someone in their 60s.

00:56:34.160 --> 00:56:37.680
So, let me show you a few things that

00:56:35.839 --> 00:56:40.640
should determine what your portfolio

00:56:37.680 --> 00:56:42.240
looks like. First, your risk tolerance.

00:56:40.640 --> 00:56:44.319
And you really need to be honest with

00:56:42.240 --> 00:56:46.559
yourself here because this matters more

00:56:44.319 --> 00:56:49.359
than most people realize. If seeing your

00:56:46.559 --> 00:56:51.920
portfolio drop 30% would make you panic

00:56:49.359 --> 00:56:54.079
and sell everything, then owning an

00:56:51.920 --> 00:56:55.839
extremely aggressive portfolio isn't

00:56:54.079 --> 00:56:57.920
going to work for you, even if it looks

00:56:55.839 --> 00:56:59.680
good on paper. You need an allocation

00:56:57.920 --> 00:57:01.680
that you can actually stick with for the

00:56:59.680 --> 00:57:03.760
long term. So, if you can genuinely

00:57:01.680 --> 00:57:06.160
watch your investments fall, understand

00:57:03.760 --> 00:57:07.839
why it's happening, and stay invested,

00:57:06.160 --> 00:57:10.319
then you can afford to take more risk

00:57:07.839 --> 00:57:12.960
for potential higher long-term rewards.

00:57:10.319 --> 00:57:15.040
Second, your time horizon. If you're

00:57:12.960 --> 00:57:17.359
investing for something 30 years away,

00:57:15.040 --> 00:57:19.040
you have time to recover from crashes,

00:57:17.359 --> 00:57:21.280
which means you can generally afford to

00:57:19.040 --> 00:57:22.799
hold more growth assets like shares. But

00:57:21.280 --> 00:57:25.760
if you're going to need the money in,

00:57:22.799 --> 00:57:27.920
let's say, 3 years, a massive market

00:57:25.760 --> 00:57:30.000
crash at exactly the wrong moment could

00:57:27.920 --> 00:57:32.079
be a disaster because you don't have

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:33.760
decades to wait for a recovery.

00:57:32.079 --> 00:57:36.559
Protecting the money you already have

00:57:33.760 --> 00:57:39.599
becomes much more important. And third,

00:57:36.559 --> 00:57:42.400
your goal. So, you need to ask yourself,

00:57:39.599 --> 00:57:44.559
what am I actually trying to achieve?

00:57:42.400 --> 00:57:46.400
because retirement, buying a house,

00:57:44.559 --> 00:57:48.400
paying for your kids' future, or just

00:57:46.400 --> 00:57:50.240
growing your money over the long term

00:57:48.400 --> 00:57:52.960
can all require slightly different

00:57:50.240 --> 00:57:54.960
approaches. Now, I can't tell you

00:57:52.960 --> 00:57:57.040
exactly what to do because I'm not a

00:57:54.960 --> 00:57:59.119
financial adviser and I don't know your

00:57:57.040 --> 00:58:00.720
specific circumstances,

00:57:59.119 --> 00:58:02.640
but if you put those three things

00:58:00.720 --> 00:58:04.640
together, they'll give you a pretty

00:58:02.640 --> 00:58:06.799
clear understanding of how to build your

00:58:04.640 --> 00:58:09.520
portfolio. If you're young, you could go

00:58:06.799 --> 00:58:13.200
for something like 75% growth index

00:58:09.520 --> 00:58:16.319
funds, 5% individual stocks, 5% crypto,

00:58:13.200 --> 00:58:18.880
and 15% bonds. And if you're older, you

00:58:16.319 --> 00:58:21.599
could go for something like 45% bonds

00:58:18.880 --> 00:58:23.839
and 55% split across different growth

00:58:21.599 --> 00:58:25.680
index funds. But of course, don't take

00:58:23.839 --> 00:58:28.160
these numbers exactly because it all

00:58:25.680 --> 00:58:30.160
depends on your specific circumstances.

00:58:28.160 --> 00:58:32.400
The most important thing is to build a

00:58:30.160 --> 00:58:35.200
diversified portfolio that makes sense

00:58:32.400 --> 00:58:37.040
for your specific goals. Now, people

00:58:35.200 --> 00:58:39.119
often think they're diversified because

00:58:37.040 --> 00:58:41.760
they own lots of different investments,

00:58:39.119 --> 00:58:43.839
but 10 different technology stocks isn't

00:58:41.760 --> 00:58:46.400
really 10 different investments because

00:58:43.839 --> 00:58:48.559
if something hits the technology sector,

00:58:46.400 --> 00:58:50.480
all 10 could fall together. Real

00:58:48.559 --> 00:58:52.400
diversification means spreading your

00:58:50.480 --> 00:58:54.640
money across investments that behave

00:58:52.400 --> 00:58:56.880
completely differently. So, different

00:58:54.640 --> 00:58:59.200
companies, different sectors, different

00:58:56.880 --> 00:59:02.400
countries, and different asset classes

00:58:59.200 --> 00:59:04.960
entirely. Look, you can never eliminate

00:59:02.400 --> 00:59:06.799
the risk completely, but diversification

00:59:04.960 --> 00:59:08.880
means one bad day doesn't have to

00:59:06.799 --> 00:59:10.720
destroy your entire portfolio. That's

00:59:08.880 --> 00:59:12.799
why I spent all that time telling you

00:59:10.720 --> 00:59:14.720
about all the different assets. Not

00:59:12.799 --> 00:59:16.480
because you need to own everything, but

00:59:14.720 --> 00:59:18.480
because each one can play a different

00:59:16.480 --> 00:59:20.799
role for you. However, building a

00:59:18.480 --> 00:59:22.240
diversified portfolio today is all well

00:59:20.799 --> 00:59:24.319
and good. But if you leave it sitting

00:59:22.240 --> 00:59:26.640
there for 10 years without ever checking

00:59:24.319 --> 00:59:28.480
in on it, you might discover that it no

00:59:26.640 --> 00:59:31.359
longer looks anything like you intended

00:59:28.480 --> 00:59:35.160
it to. That's called drift. So, let me

00:59:31.359 --> 00:59:35.160
show you how to fix it.

00:59:36.000 --> 00:59:40.640
Let's say [music] you set yourself up

00:59:37.440 --> 00:59:42.960
with a portfolio of 80% stocks and 20%

00:59:40.640 --> 00:59:44.960
bonds. Then stocks have a brilliant year

00:59:42.960 --> 00:59:47.520
and shoot up. Suddenly, without you

00:59:44.960 --> 00:59:49.680
actually doing anything, you're at 90%

00:59:47.520 --> 00:59:51.680
stocks because your winners grew so much

00:59:49.680 --> 00:59:53.839
that they threw your whole portfolio out

00:59:51.680 --> 00:59:55.520
of balance. You're now taking on more

00:59:53.839 --> 00:59:57.119
risk than you originally decided you

00:59:55.520 --> 00:59:58.880
were comfortable with. This actually

00:59:57.119 --> 01:00:01.359
happens more often than you'd probably

00:59:58.880 --> 01:00:02.960
think. And on its own, it's not really a

01:00:01.359 --> 01:00:04.720
problem as long as you know how to

01:00:02.960 --> 01:00:06.880
rebalance your portfolio and get

01:00:04.720 --> 01:00:09.359
yourself back on target. So, let me show

01:00:06.880 --> 01:00:10.640
you two easy ways to do it. First, you

01:00:09.359 --> 01:00:12.799
can sell a little bit of the stuff

01:00:10.640 --> 01:00:14.799
that's grown too big and use that money

01:00:12.799 --> 01:00:16.799
to buy more of the stuff that's lagging

01:00:14.799 --> 01:00:18.720
behind. But, I personally wouldn't

01:00:16.799 --> 01:00:20.640
recommend doing this, unless of course

01:00:18.720 --> 01:00:22.720
you want to reduce your exposure to the

01:00:20.640 --> 01:00:24.160
stocks that are performing well because

01:00:22.720 --> 01:00:26.079
you may think they're going to dip in

01:00:24.160 --> 01:00:28.480
the future. The second approach, which

01:00:26.079 --> 01:00:30.319
in my opinion is the best way, is to

01:00:28.480 --> 01:00:32.319
allocate all of your future monthly

01:00:30.319 --> 01:00:34.799
contributions to the parts of your

01:00:32.319 --> 01:00:36.799
portfolio that have fallen behind until

01:00:34.799 --> 01:00:38.400
the balance is restored. Doing it this

01:00:36.799 --> 01:00:40.480
way means you can rebalance the

01:00:38.400 --> 01:00:42.480
portfolio without having to sell off any

01:00:40.480 --> 01:00:44.880
of your winning stocks and potentially

01:00:42.480 --> 01:00:46.480
be liable for capital gains taxes

01:00:44.880 --> 01:00:48.640
depending on the account type you're

01:00:46.480 --> 01:00:51.040
using. Look, you don't need to obsess

01:00:48.640 --> 01:00:52.960
over tiny movements. The whole point of

01:00:51.040 --> 01:00:54.559
balancing your portfolio is just to keep

01:00:52.960 --> 01:00:57.119
it roughly aligned with the level of

01:00:54.559 --> 01:01:00.000
risk you originally chose, not to hit an

01:00:57.119 --> 01:01:02.079
exact percentage every single week. For

01:01:00.000 --> 01:01:04.319
most long-term investors, checking once

01:01:02.079 --> 01:01:06.960
or twice a year is more than enough. So,

01:01:04.319 --> 01:01:09.359
set a date, tidy it up, then move on

01:01:06.960 --> 01:01:11.119
with your life. But speaking of selling,

01:01:09.359 --> 01:01:13.599
let's move on to one of the hardest

01:01:11.119 --> 01:01:15.957
skills in investing. Knowing when to

01:01:13.599 --> 01:01:15.957
sell. [music]

01:01:17.119 --> 01:01:21.797
Buying an investment is easy because if

01:01:19.760 --> 01:01:22.400
I'm being honest, it's very exciting.

01:01:21.797 --> 01:01:24.160
[music]

01:01:22.400 --> 01:01:26.400
But selling, on the other hand, is where

01:01:24.160 --> 01:01:29.040
most people fall short. Either clinging

01:01:26.400 --> 01:01:31.119
onto a disaster out of pride or dumping

01:01:29.040 --> 01:01:32.799
a potential winner in a state of panic.

01:01:31.119 --> 01:01:35.359
So, let me give you a clear framework

01:01:32.799 --> 01:01:37.760
for when to actually sell any asset. So,

01:01:35.359 --> 01:01:39.599
it's a clear decision and not driven by

01:01:37.760 --> 01:01:42.079
emotion. There are really only ever

01:01:39.599 --> 01:01:44.559
three good reasons to sell. The first is

01:01:42.079 --> 01:01:46.480
that the momentum's died. This is mostly

01:01:44.559 --> 01:01:49.119
for hyped driven assets, kind of like

01:01:46.480 --> 01:01:50.799
when AMC and GameStop were pumping a few

01:01:49.119 --> 01:01:52.720
years ago. And if you were still hanging

01:01:50.799 --> 01:01:54.720
on to that stock, you'd have done very

01:01:52.720 --> 01:01:56.559
badly to say the least. The second

01:01:54.720 --> 01:01:58.240
reason is that you found a genuinely

01:01:56.559 --> 01:01:59.920
better use for that money. When it comes

01:01:58.240 --> 01:02:02.319
to selling an investment, a lot of

01:01:59.920 --> 01:02:04.559
people like to preach diamond hands and

01:02:02.319 --> 01:02:06.160
make it out like selling is a bad thing.

01:02:04.559 --> 01:02:08.240
But if you've clearly found a bigger

01:02:06.160 --> 01:02:10.160
opportunity for your money, selling to

01:02:08.240 --> 01:02:12.880
reallocate that money is a smart

01:02:10.160 --> 01:02:14.720
decision. And the third reason is if the

01:02:12.880 --> 01:02:17.440
reason you invested has completely

01:02:14.720 --> 01:02:19.839
changed. Look, over time businesses

01:02:17.440 --> 01:02:22.079
change, leadership changes, and the

01:02:19.839 --> 01:02:24.240
market shifts. Even Warren Buffett, who

01:02:22.079 --> 01:02:26.240
famously loves to hold forever, still

01:02:24.240 --> 01:02:28.720
dumped all of his airline stocks when

01:02:26.240 --> 01:02:30.880
COVID hit. In my opinion, this whole

01:02:28.720 --> 01:02:33.680
diamond hands and hold no matter what

01:02:30.880 --> 01:02:36.160
idea is completely stupid. I'll be

01:02:33.680 --> 01:02:38.559
honest. I hate selling a stock just as

01:02:36.160 --> 01:02:40.640
much as the next person. But loyalty to

01:02:38.559 --> 01:02:42.799
an investment that fundamentally is

01:02:40.640 --> 01:02:44.559
broken. It's just stubbornness and will

01:02:42.799 --> 01:02:46.799
cost you a lot of money over the long

01:02:44.559 --> 01:02:48.720
term. Now, when you do decide to sell,

01:02:46.799 --> 01:02:51.599
this is how you do it. If we head over

01:02:48.720 --> 01:02:53.440
to it Toro and click this little pi

01:02:51.599 --> 01:02:56.000
icon, that will take us to our

01:02:53.440 --> 01:02:58.960
portfolio. From here, we can just simply

01:02:56.000 --> 01:03:02.559
select the stock we want to sell. Swipe

01:02:58.960 --> 01:03:05.599
to the left and tap this red tab. Tick

01:03:02.559 --> 01:03:07.839
the box just here and then just select

01:03:05.599 --> 01:03:10.400
the amount that we'd like to sell and

01:03:07.839 --> 01:03:12.079
boom, it's done. As simple as that. But

01:03:10.400 --> 01:03:14.160
remember, unless you're investing in a

01:03:12.079 --> 01:03:16.240
tax efficient account, the second you

01:03:14.160 --> 01:03:19.200
press sell, you could be liable for

01:03:16.240 --> 01:03:20.960
capital gains taxes on your profit. So,

01:03:19.200 --> 01:03:24.338
let me show you how to keep as much of

01:03:20.960 --> 01:03:24.338
your money as possible. [music]

01:03:25.520 --> 01:03:29.760
I know the word tax makes your eyes roll

01:03:27.920 --> 01:03:31.599
in the back of your head, but stick with

01:03:29.760 --> 01:03:33.520
me for a few minutes because

01:03:31.599 --> 01:03:35.119
understanding how it works means you can

01:03:33.520 --> 01:03:37.039
keep thousands of dollars in your own

01:03:35.119 --> 01:03:39.119
pocket. And it's really not as

01:03:37.039 --> 01:03:41.440
complicated as it sounds. Now, the most

01:03:39.119 --> 01:03:43.760
reassuring part of all this is that

01:03:41.440 --> 01:03:45.920
you're not taxed on your gains until you

01:03:43.760 --> 01:03:48.160
actually sell. If your investments go up

01:03:45.920 --> 01:03:50.720
and you just hold them, that growth is

01:03:48.160 --> 01:03:52.720
called an unrealized gain and the tax

01:03:50.720 --> 01:03:54.559
man can't touch it. It's only when you

01:03:52.720 --> 01:03:56.880
sell a stock and lock in the profit,

01:03:54.559 --> 01:03:58.960
which is a realized gain, that the tax

01:03:56.880 --> 01:04:01.039
man comes into play. So, holding a stock

01:03:58.960 --> 01:04:03.280
for the long term already delays and

01:04:01.039 --> 01:04:06.079
possibly reduces your tax bill. But if

01:04:03.280 --> 01:04:08.559
you do decide to sell, how to taxes

01:04:06.079 --> 01:04:10.400
actually work? Well, in the US, if

01:04:08.559 --> 01:04:12.640
you've held an investment for a year or

01:04:10.400 --> 01:04:15.280
less, your profit is generally treated

01:04:12.640 --> 01:04:17.119
as a short-term capital gain and taxed

01:04:15.280 --> 01:04:19.200
at your ordinary income rate. If you

01:04:17.119 --> 01:04:21.680
hold for more than a year, it becomes a

01:04:19.200 --> 01:04:25.760
long-term capital gain which can qualify

01:04:21.680 --> 01:04:27.599
for the much lower 0, 15, or 20% rates

01:04:25.760 --> 01:04:29.520
depending on your taxable income and

01:04:27.599 --> 01:04:31.440
filing status. So, have a look at your

01:04:29.520 --> 01:04:33.200
local tax laws to see which one you

01:04:31.440 --> 01:04:36.160
might fit into. And if you're from the

01:04:33.200 --> 01:04:38.240
UK, the system works a bit differently.

01:04:36.160 --> 01:04:40.319
There's no special lower rate just for

01:04:38.240 --> 01:04:43.119
holding a share for a year or more. and

01:04:40.319 --> 01:04:45.520
instead you generally get a £3,000

01:04:43.119 --> 01:04:47.760
capital gains tax annual allowance and

01:04:45.520 --> 01:04:51.039
gains above that are typically taxed at

01:04:47.760 --> 01:04:53.359
18 to 24% depending on your income. But

01:04:51.039 --> 01:04:55.440
if I'm being honest, this is exactly why

01:04:53.359 --> 01:04:57.680
the tax advantage accounts we spoke

01:04:55.440 --> 01:05:00.000
about earlier are so important because

01:04:57.680 --> 01:05:02.480
inside those accounts, most of this tax

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:05.039
just doesn't apply. So that's how tax

01:05:02.480 --> 01:05:09.140
works. Now let me show you how to

01:05:05.039 --> 01:05:09.140
legally pay less of it. [music]

01:05:09.839 --> 01:05:13.440
Now, I just want to start this by saying

01:05:11.680 --> 01:05:15.359
I'm not a tax advisor [music] and you

01:05:13.440 --> 01:05:17.600
should seek your own individual tax

01:05:15.359 --> 01:05:19.520
advice, but let me show you a few of the

01:05:17.600 --> 01:05:21.760
[music] strategies experts use to pay

01:05:19.520 --> 01:05:24.559
less tax. The main trick is called tax

01:05:21.760 --> 01:05:26.640
loss harvesting. It sounds complicated,

01:05:24.559 --> 01:05:28.480
but all it really means is that if you

01:05:26.640 --> 01:05:30.720
have an investment that's lost a lot of

01:05:28.480 --> 01:05:32.960
value, you can deliberately sell it to

01:05:30.720 --> 01:05:34.799
realize that loss. In the US, that can

01:05:32.960 --> 01:05:36.799
be used to offset capital gains from

01:05:34.799 --> 01:05:38.400
investments that have gone up in value,

01:05:36.799 --> 01:05:40.480
reducing the amount of gain you're

01:05:38.400 --> 01:05:43.119
actually taxed on. You're basically

01:05:40.480 --> 01:05:44.960
turning a losing investment into a tax

01:05:43.119 --> 01:05:47.119
benefit. However, you have to watch out

01:05:44.960 --> 01:05:49.359
for the wash sale rule, which means if

01:05:47.119 --> 01:05:51.920
you sell an investment at a loss and buy

01:05:49.359 --> 01:05:54.079
an identical investment within 30 days,

01:05:51.920 --> 01:05:56.000
the IRS can disallow it. You can't just

01:05:54.079 --> 01:05:57.760
sell something on a Monday, claim the

01:05:56.000 --> 01:06:00.400
loss, and then buy the exact same thing

01:05:57.760 --> 01:06:02.640
again on a Tuesday. The UK also has its

01:06:00.400 --> 01:06:04.559
own 30-day bed and breakfasting rule,

01:06:02.640 --> 01:06:07.280
which can affect how the loss is

01:06:04.559 --> 01:06:09.680
calculated. If you sell shares and buy

01:06:07.280 --> 01:06:12.079
the same class of shares again within 30

01:06:09.680 --> 01:06:13.839
days, the rules are different. So, this

01:06:12.079 --> 01:06:15.520
is an area where you really need to know

01:06:13.839 --> 01:06:17.599
which country's tax system you're

01:06:15.520 --> 01:06:19.280
investing under. Beyond that, there's

01:06:17.599 --> 01:06:21.440
two simple habits that have served me

01:06:19.280 --> 01:06:23.280
well over the years. First, always use

01:06:21.440 --> 01:06:25.440
your tax efficient accounts before

01:06:23.280 --> 01:06:27.599
piling into a taxable account. For

01:06:25.440 --> 01:06:29.920
example, in the UK, as we already

01:06:27.599 --> 01:06:31.839
discussed, a stocks and shares ISA can

01:06:29.920 --> 01:06:34.319
shelter investments from capital gains

01:06:31.839 --> 01:06:36.160
tax and dividend tax. So there's little

01:06:34.319 --> 01:06:38.480
reason to create a taxable gain

01:06:36.160 --> 01:06:40.559
unnecessarily when you have an available

01:06:38.480 --> 01:06:42.240
ISE or allowance. In the US,

01:06:40.559 --> 01:06:44.000
unfortunately, it's not as

01:06:42.240 --> 01:06:46.400
straightforward as this because when

01:06:44.000 --> 01:06:48.079
investing in taxefficient accounts, you

01:06:46.400 --> 01:06:50.319
often can't withdraw the money until

01:06:48.079 --> 01:06:52.640
retirement without a penalty. But it's

01:06:50.319 --> 01:06:54.720
still worth being aware of. And second,

01:06:52.640 --> 01:06:56.880
don't try to save on tax when it's not

01:06:54.720 --> 01:06:59.039
necessary. I've watched people make

01:06:56.880 --> 01:07:01.200
genuinely bad investment decisions

01:06:59.039 --> 01:07:04.160
purely to save a little bit of money on

01:07:01.200 --> 01:07:06.319
tax and end up worse overall. Save on

01:07:04.160 --> 01:07:08.240
tax where it's easy and obvious, but

01:07:06.319 --> 01:07:10.720
never let the tax bill drive the whole

01:07:08.240 --> 01:07:12.960
investment decision. Sometimes paying

01:07:10.720 --> 01:07:15.359
some tax on a profitable investment is

01:07:12.960 --> 01:07:17.200
